{%- comment -%}
  Quick add-to-cart control for a product card.
  Rendered in two places by aria-product-card.liquid: inside the actions row (default
  card) or pinned inside the media (compact card). Extracted so both call sites share
  one copy of the <product-form> wiring.

  Params: product, section_id, is_sold_out, icon (optional), variant_id (optional),
          hard_disable (optional), product_name (optional)

  `product_name` is the name the button's accessible label reads out. Call sites that show a
  supplier-free title (aria-strip-supplier) pass that same title so the hidden label does not
  name the supplier either. Defaults to `product.title`.

  `hard_disable` emits the NATIVE `disabled` attribute alongside `aria-disabled` when the
  product is sold out. `aria-disabled` alone stops product-form.js, but any third-party
  script that listens for clicks on add-to-cart buttons and posts `/cart/add.js` itself
  ignores it. `pointer-events: none` blocks a real mouse
  click but not a keyboard Enter on a focused button, so only a natively disabled button —
  which cannot be focused or activated at all — closes the hole for a sold-out product.
  Opt-in, so existing call sites render byte-identically.

  `variant_id` lets a call site pin the variant this form starts on. aria-product-card
  passes the variant its ringed colour swatch resolves to, so the ring and the posted
  variant agree on first load. Omitted everywhere else, which keeps the previous default
  (`selected_or_first_available_variant`) for the mega menu and Instagram call sites.

  `icon` lets a call site swap the glyph without forking this snippet. The mega menu passes
  V4's own `mega-cart-02`; everywhere else keeps the theme's `cart`.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign quick_add_name = product_name | default: product.title -%}
<product-form class="aria-product-card__quick-add">
  <form
    method="post"
    action="{{ routes.cart_add_url }}"
    id="AriaQuickAdd-{{ section_id }}-{{ product.id }}"
    accept-charset="UTF-8"
    class="aria-product-card__quick-add-form"
    data-hide-errors="true"
  >
    {%- liquid
      assign quick_add_variant_id = variant_id
      if quick_add_variant_id == blank
        assign quick_add_variant_id = product.selected_or_first_available_variant.id
      endif
    -%}
    <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ quick_add_variant_id }}">
    <input type="hidden" name="quantity" value="1">
    <button
      type="submit"
      name="add"
      class="aria-product-card__cart-btn"
      aria-haspopup="dialog"
      {% if is_sold_out %}
        aria-disabled="true"
        {% if hard_disable %}
          disabled
        {% endif %}
      {% endif %}
      aria-label="{{ 'aria_theme.product_card.add_to_cart_named' | t: product_name: quick_add_name }}"
    >
      {%- comment -%}
        Resolved with `assign` first rather than `name: icon | default: 'cart'`. Filters on a
        `render` argument work at runtime but `theme check` rejects them
        (UnsupportedFilterArguments), and the two-line form is what the rule asks for.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign quick_add_icon = icon
        if quick_add_icon == blank
          assign quick_add_icon = 'cart'
        endif
      -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Hidden everywhere by default (component-aria-product-card.css) — this button is
        icon-only at every other call site (mega menu, Instagram feed, the compact card,
        the desktop/tablet product card). One consumer (the PDP "buy alongside" mobile
        carousel, section-aria-pdp-product-carousel.css) shows it, matching a Figma frame
        where the mobile card's action row is a single full-width "Add to Cart" button
        rather than the desktop View Product + icon pair. Real text rather than a CSS
        `content` string so it still comes from the existing locale key.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <span class="aria-product-card__cart-label">{{ 'aria_theme.product_card.add_to_cart' | t }}</span>
      {%- render 'aria-icon', name: quick_add_icon -%}
      <span class="loading__spinner hidden" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </button>
  </form>
</product-form>
